window.ROADMAP = {"weeks": [{"n": 1, "title": "Python foundations", "intro": "Build a typed, testable Python client and a clean repository.", "days": [{"n": 1, "title": "Setup", "tasks": ["Check a maintained Python version supported by your libraries, Git, editor and Docker.", "Create genai-engineer-journey, a virtual environment, README and .gitignore.", "Run a hello script."], "page": 6}, {"n": 2, "title": "Types and collections", "tasks": ["Practice functions, lists/dicts, type hints and exceptions.", "Transform a small JSON payload into typed records; explain Kotlin-to-Python differences.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 6}, {"n": 3, "title": "Models and structure", "tasks": ["Use dataclasses and Pydantic validation.", "Split config, client and models into modules; reject malformed input with a useful error.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 6}, {"n": 4, "title": "HTTP and configuration", "tasks": ["Call a public JSON API with a timeout.", "Load configuration from environment variables; add .env.example with placeholders and keep secrets out of Git.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 7}, {"n": 5, "title": "Async and tests", "tasks": ["Use an async HTTP client for two independent requests.", "Write pytest cases for success, timeout and invalid data using mocks.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 7}, {"n": 6, "title": "Catch-up and ship", "tasks": ["Finish the highest-priority unfinished core task; add no new topic.", "Make a CLI that fetches and summarizes public data. Add setup/run instructions and a sample output; reproduce it in a fresh environment.", "Commit evidence and check the weekly acceptance gate."], "page": 7}, {"n": 7, "title": "30-minute mentor review, then rest", "tasks": ["Record hours, evidence, one lesson and the next action on the weekly review sheet."], "page": 7}], "gate": "A fresh checkout runs the CLI; at least 3 meaningful failure/success tests pass; you can explain await, validation and exception flow.", "fallback": "Keep one public API and a CLI; omit extra libraries.", "page": 6}, {"n": 2, "title": "FastAPI backend", "intro": "Build the backend foundation used by all three portfolio projects.", "days": [{"n": 1, "title": "Endpoints", "tasks": ["Create /health and CRUD endpoints for documents metadata.", "Define request and response models; inspect generated OpenAPI.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 8}, {"n": 2, "title": "Persistence", "tasks": ["Add PostgreSQL and migrations.", "Store document IDs and ownership; avoid placing database logic directly in routes.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 8}, {"n": 3, "title": "Private demo authentication", "tasks": ["Follow the FastAPI JWT/password tutorial with password hashing and two seeded synthetic users.", "Protect one endpoint and reject expired or invalid tokens.", "Keep public signup out of scope."], "page": 8}, {"n": 4, "title": "Authorization", "tasks": ["Scope queries to the authenticated owner.", "Verify that user B cannot read or delete user A's document by changing an ID.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 9}, {"n": 5, "title": "Essential error handling", "tasks": ["Bound request inputs and return consistent errors.", "Test invalid input, missing login and cross-user access.", "Optional: pagination after these tests pass."], "page": 9}, {"n": 6, "title": "Catch-up and ship", "tasks": ["Finish the highest-priority unfinished core task; add no new topic.", "Document local setup and example requests. Add a CI test job; demonstrate create, list and forbidden access.", "Commit evidence and check the weekly acceptance gate."], "page": 9}, {"n": 7, "title": "30-minute mentor review, then rest", "tasks": ["Record hours, evidence, one lesson and the next action on the weekly review sheet."], "page": 9}], "gate": "CRUD persists across restart; protected routes reject unauthenticated access; cross-user tests pass; CI can run without real model credentials.", "fallback": "Keep two seeded users and ownership tests; postpone pagination and registration.", "page": 8}, {"n": 3, "title": "LLM APIs and tool calling", "intro": "Create a small model-backed service before introducing orchestration.", "days": [{"n": 1, "title": "First integration", "tasks": ["Use Claude as the primary provider.", "Read the current official SDK quickstart, put credentials on the server and implement /chat with a timeout.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 10}, {"n": 2, "title": "Structured responses", "tasks": ["Define a schema for a support summary.", "Validate returned data; distinguish invalid output, refusal and transient provider failures.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 10}, {"n": 3, "title": "Streaming and budgets", "tasks": ["Stream text to a simple client.", "Handle cancellation, token limits and bounded conversation history; record usage and elapsed time.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 10}, {"n": 4, "title": "Tools", "tasks": ["Implement two safe tools: search local FAQ and calculate a total.", "Validate arguments and return structured tool errors; never eval model text.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 11}, {"n": 5, "title": "Bounded tool loop", "tasks": ["Execute requested tools and return results to the model.", "Limit steps and retries; test unknown tools, invalid arguments and tool timeout.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 11}, {"n": 6, "title": "Catch-up and ship", "tasks": ["Finish the highest-priority unfinished core task; add no new topic.", "Finish the Claude endpoint and bounded tool loop; document setup and one failure example. Optional: compare 10 fixed prompts with an OpenAI adapter after the core gate passes.", "Commit evidence and check the weekly acceptance gate."], "page": 11}, {"n": 7, "title": "30-minute mentor review, then rest", "tasks": ["Record hours, evidence, one lesson and the next action on the weekly review sheet."], "page": 11}], "gate": "Schema outputs validate on your fixture set; tool execution is bounded; errors are visible; no keys appear in code or logs. OpenAI comparison may be mocked if unfunded.", "fallback": "Keep one provider, validated output and two tools; postpone streaming and provider comparison.", "page": 10}, {"n": 4, "title": "RAG: ingestion and retrieval", "intro": "Start Enterprise RAG Assistant with traceable source documents.", "days": [{"n": 1, "title": "Dataset and split", "tasks": ["Collect 20 public or synthetic policy documents.", "Label 40 questions with source IDs: 28 answerable, 8 unanswerable and 4 adversarial.", "Save 25 development cases (18/5/2) and 15 held-out cases (10/3/2) before any tuning; keep held-out questions out of experiments."], "page": 12}, {"n": 2, "title": "Parsing", "tasks": ["Extract text and preserve title, source, section/page and owner metadata.", "Log parsing failures; exclude unreadable or empty files.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 12}, {"n": 3, "title": "Chunking", "tasks": ["Implement a simple section-aware chunker.", "Try two chunk sizes and overlaps; inspect 10 chunks manually for context loss.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 12}, {"n": 4, "title": "Embeddings and storage", "tasks": ["Choose one embedding model and PostgreSQL + pgvector.", "Store model/version metadata; batch embeddings and upsert stable chunk IDs.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 13}, {"n": 5, "title": "Retrieval", "tasks": ["Build top-k search with authorization filters.", "Return source IDs and scores; inspect results for 10 labeled questions before generation.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 13}, {"n": 6, "title": "Catch-up and ship", "tasks": ["Finish the highest-priority unfinished core task; add no new topic.", "Make ingestion repeatable and duplicate-safe. Add delete/reindex behavior and document the pipeline; keep evaluation fixtures versioned.", "Commit evidence and check the weekly acceptance gate."], "page": 13}, {"n": 7, "title": "30-minute mentor review, then rest", "tasks": ["Record hours, evidence, one lesson and the next action on the weekly review sheet."], "page": 13}], "gate": "Every chunk resolves to a source; re-ingestion avoids duplicates; deleting a document removes retrievable chunks; retrieval cannot cross owner boundaries.", "fallback": "Use text/Markdown documents first; postpone OCR and complex PDF parsing.", "page": 12}, {"n": 5, "title": "RAG: answers and citations", "intro": "Turn retrieval into a trustworthy assistant and publish a first demo.", "days": [{"n": 1, "title": "Grounded answers", "tasks": ["Build /ask using retrieved context.", "Require citations by chunk ID and an explicit insufficient-evidence response.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 14}, {"n": 2, "title": "Citation validation", "tasks": ["Resolve citations to original source locations.", "Reject unknown citation IDs; inspect whether each cited passage actually supports its claim.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 14}, {"n": 3, "title": "Baseline comparison", "tasks": ["Measure vector retrieval on development questions only.", "Inspect its misses and record recall@5 with a source-ID matching rule.", "Optional: compare lexical or hybrid retrieval if the baseline is already working."], "page": 14}, {"n": 4, "title": "One improvement", "tasks": ["Fix the largest development-set retrieval failure.", "Save before/after results using the same configuration record.", "Optional: evaluate a reranker and its extra latency/cost."], "page": 15}, {"n": 5, "title": "Safety and UI", "tasks": ["Treat document text as untrusted data.", "Test injected instructions, unsupported questions and cross-user access; add a minimal chat UI with source previews.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 15}, {"n": 6, "title": "Catch-up and ship", "tasks": ["Finish the highest-priority unfinished core task; add no new topic.", "Record a 2-3 minute demo: normal answer, source inspection, abstention and an attack attempt. Write the design decisions and current limitations.", "Commit evidence and check the weekly acceptance gate."], "page": 15}, {"n": 7, "title": "30-minute mentor review, then rest", "tasks": ["Record hours, evidence, one lesson and the next action on the weekly review sheet."], "page": 15}], "gate": "Demo handles answerable and unanswerable questions; citations open the right source; evidence and attack cases are recorded. Call it a prototype until operations are hardened.", "fallback": "Keep vector search and source previews; postpone hybrid search and reranking.", "page": 14}, {"n": 6, "title": "Evaluation and RAG hardening", "intro": "Measure the assistant and turn findings into an engineering report.", "days": [{"n": 1, "title": "Audit the evaluation contract", "tasks": ["Verify that the week-4 held-out questions have not been used for tuning.", "Freeze the selected configuration and labels.", "If test questions were exposed, create a fresh independently labeled holdout and document the change."], "page": 16}, {"n": 2, "title": "Retrieval metrics", "tasks": ["Calculate recall@5 against expected evidence chunks or source IDs.", "Define the matching unit and inspect every miss.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 16}, {"n": 3, "title": "Answer metrics", "tasks": ["Score correctness, citation support and abstention with a written rubric.", "Use human review for the small set; audit any model-judge scores.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 16}, {"n": 4, "title": "Final held-out run", "tasks": ["Compare candidate settings on development cases only and select one.", "Run baseline and selected configuration on the untouched held-out set once.", "Report counts and examples; any later fixes need a new test set for a fresh generalization claim."], "page": 17}, {"n": 5, "title": "Latency and cost", "tasks": ["Record per-request input/output usage, retrieval time, model time and total time.", "Run at least 30 timed requests; label p95 estimates as a small sample.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 17}, {"n": 6, "title": "Catch-up and ship", "tasks": ["Finish the highest-priority unfinished core task; add no new topic.", "Finish the evaluation report with versions, raw counts and three failure analyses. Add development regression cases for fixes. Preserve the original held-out result; do not quietly retune it.", "Commit evidence and check the weekly acceptance gate."], "page": 17}, {"n": 7, "title": "30-minute mentor review, then rest", "tasks": ["Record hours, evidence, one lesson and the next action on the weekly review sheet."], "page": 17}], "gate": "Publish baseline versus selected configuration, honest held-out results and at least 3 failure analyses. Small benchmark results are evidence, not a universal accuracy claim.", "fallback": "Keep human scoring on the small fixture set; postpone automated model judges.", "page": 16}, {"n": 7, "title": "LangGraph and agent workflows", "intro": "Start Agentic AI Platform as a bounded workflow, reusing RAG.", "days": [{"n": 1, "title": "Choose the workflow", "tasks": ["Define a support triage flow: classify issue, retrieve policy, draft response, request approval, create a sandbox ticket.", "Compare it with a deterministic pipeline.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 18}, {"n": 2, "title": "State and nodes", "tasks": ["Implement typed state and LangGraph nodes.", "Track request ID, tool results and status; make routing conditions explicit.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 18}, {"n": 3, "title": "Tool contracts", "tasks": ["Add retrieve_policy, lookup_ticket and create_sandbox_ticket.", "Validate schemas and permissions; use synthetic data and sandbox integrations.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 18}, {"n": 4, "title": "Checkpointing", "tasks": ["Persist state and resume after interruption.", "Simulate a crash between steps; inspect what is re-executed.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 19}, {"n": 5, "title": "Approval and idempotency", "tasks": ["Pause before ticket creation.", "Bind approval to the exact proposed action; add an idempotency key to prevent duplicate side effects.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 19}, {"n": 6, "title": "Catch-up and ship", "tasks": ["Finish the highest-priority unfinished core task; add no new topic.", "Demonstrate successful routing, denial and resume. Add a trace showing each state transition; explain why a graph was justified.", "Commit evidence and check the weekly acceptance gate."], "page": 19}, {"n": 7, "title": "30-minute mentor review, then rest", "tasks": ["Record hours, evidence, one lesson and the next action on the weekly review sheet."], "page": 19}], "gate": "The workflow resumes from stored state; writes require explicit approval; replay does not create duplicate tickets; deterministic alternatives are documented.", "fallback": "Use one deterministic route plus one model decision; avoid multiple agents.", "page": 18}, {"n": 8, "title": "Agent reliability and observability", "intro": "Make agent failures controlled, diagnosable and measurable.", "days": [{"n": 1, "title": "Budgets", "tasks": ["Add maximum steps, wall-clock deadline, tool concurrency limit and per-run cost guard.", "Show a clear stop reason when a budget is hit.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 20}, {"n": 2, "title": "Recovery", "tasks": ["Retry transient reads with backoff.", "Do not blindly retry writes; test partial failure, duplicate requests and provider outage.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 20}, {"n": 3, "title": "Traces", "tasks": ["Capture graph node, tool name, latency, status and correlation ID.", "Redact sensitive inputs and store only necessary content.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 20}, {"n": 4, "title": "Scenario evaluation", "tasks": ["Create 20 fixed scenarios: normal paths, malicious tool text, unauthorized access, timeout, rejected approval and repeated requests.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 21}, {"n": 5, "title": "Inspect and improve", "tasks": ["Compare scenario success and tool-call correctness before/after one change.", "Separate model mistakes from orchestration and permission failures.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 21}, {"n": 6, "title": "Catch-up and ship", "tasks": ["Finish the highest-priority unfinished core task; add no new topic.", "Expose create-run, run-status and resume endpoints. Publish a demo and architecture diagram with failure handling and limitations.", "Commit evidence and check the weekly acceptance gate."], "page": 21}, {"n": 7, "title": "30-minute mentor review, then rest", "tasks": ["Record hours, evidence, one lesson and the next action on the weekly review sheet."], "page": 21}], "gate": "All critical authorization/approval cases pass; 20-scenario report includes failures; every run has a trace and final status; budgets reliably terminate loops.", "fallback": "Keep JSON traces and 20 scenarios; postpone dashboards.", "page": 20}, {"n": 9, "title": "MCP integrations", "intro": "Connect the agent to external tools through a small, secure MCP server.", "days": [{"n": 1, "title": "Protocol basics", "tasks": ["Learn host, client and server roles; distinguish tools, resources and prompts.", "Trace one request using a local MCP development client.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 22}, {"n": 2, "title": "Server", "tasks": ["Build a local Python MCP server exposing search_policy and get_ticket with typed inputs and structured results.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 22}, {"n": 3, "title": "Client integration", "tasks": ["Connect your existing agent to this server.", "Reuse tool validation and limits; handle missing server and tool errors gracefully.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 22}, {"n": 4, "title": "Trust boundaries", "tasks": ["Allowlist tools and executable paths, minimize scopes and validate outputs.", "Keep approvals for writes; distinguish local stdio from remote HTTP auth needs.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 23}, {"n": 5, "title": "Security exercise", "tasks": ["Test malicious tool descriptions/results, unauthorized IDs and server disconnect.", "For remote HTTP, study official authorization guidance; do not pass unrelated tokens through.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 23}, {"n": 6, "title": "Catch-up and ship", "tasks": ["Finish the highest-priority unfinished core task; add no new topic.", "Publish setup, transport, permissions and a short sequence diagram. Record a working agent-to-MCP demo; list remaining remote-production requirements.", "Commit evidence and check the weekly acceptance gate."], "page": 23}, {"n": 7, "title": "30-minute mentor review, then rest", "tasks": ["Record hours, evidence, one lesson and the next action on the weekly review sheet."], "page": 23}], "gate": "Two MCP tools work end-to-end; denied data stays denied; failure handling is tested; README states transport and trust assumptions. Remote hosting is optional.", "fallback": "Use local stdio with two read tools; postpone remote auth and hosting.", "page": 22}, {"n": 10, "title": "Docker and deployment", "intro": "Operate the RAG and agent backend in a reproducible environment.", "days": [{"n": 1, "title": "Containers", "tasks": ["Create a Docker image and Compose setup with PostgreSQL.", "Run as non-root, pin dependencies and keep secrets outside image layers.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 24}, {"n": 2, "title": "One staging deployment", "tasks": ["Prepare one Render container service and a managed PostgreSQL instance after checking cost and pgvector availability.", "Deploy with TLS and server-side secrets if funded.", "Otherwise record a reproducible local Docker demo and label hosting incomplete."], "page": 24}, {"n": 3, "title": "Security checks", "tasks": ["Set upload/type/size limits, rate limits, restricted CORS and safe outbound destinations.", "Test auth, document ownership and approval on the deployed service.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 24}, {"n": 4, "title": "Minimum operations", "tasks": ["Add request IDs, structured error logs and health/readiness endpoints.", "Record request latency and model usage with sensitive content redacted.", "Optional: connect an OpenTelemetry viewer and alerts."], "page": 25}, {"n": 5, "title": "Recovery", "tasks": ["Run migrations; test backup/restore on disposable data and rollback to a previous image.", "Set spend alerts and a hard app-side request budget.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 25}, {"n": 6, "title": "Catch-up and ship", "tasks": ["Finish the highest-priority unfinished core task; add no new topic.", "Finish the deployment runbook and smoke test. Record backup/restore and previous-image rollback evidence from local disposable infrastructure if staging time is limited. State exactly which environment was tested.", "Commit evidence and check the weekly acceptance gate."], "page": 25}, {"n": 7, "title": "30-minute mentor review, then rest", "tasks": ["Record hours, evidence, one lesson and the next action on the weekly review sheet."], "page": 25}], "gate": "A reviewer can run Docker locally; staging is reachable or demonstrated privately; restart preserves data; backup restore and rollback are exercised.", "fallback": "A local reproducible release is acceptable evidence; mark public hosting unfinished.", "page": 24}, {"n": 11, "title": "Android + AI and portfolio", "intro": "Use Android experience to make your profile distinctive.", "days": [{"n": 1, "title": "Scope the mobile client", "tasks": ["Build a Kotlin/Compose client for your existing RAG API: login, question entry, answer and source list.", "Reuse the backend instead of creating a third AI service.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 26}, {"n": 2, "title": "Async UX", "tasks": ["Use coroutines and lifecycle-aware state.", "Support loading, cancellation, retry and clear network errors; handle streamed output if time allows.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 26}, {"n": 3, "title": "Mobile security", "tasks": ["Keep model API keys on the backend.", "Store session credentials appropriately; clear user state at logout and avoid logging sensitive answers.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 26}, {"n": 4, "title": "Device validation", "tasks": ["Test slow network, rotation, expired login and long answers.", "Check accessibility and source navigation; record emulator/device results.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 27}, {"n": 5, "title": "Portfolio essentials", "tasks": ["Update existing README files with verified setup and current limitations.", "Add one architecture diagram and one evaluation table per backend project.", "Optional: create a polished portfolio website later."], "page": 27}, {"n": 6, "title": "Catch-up and ship", "tasks": ["Finish the highest-priority unfinished core task; add no new topic.", "Finish the Android build and record a 90-second demo. Verify all three project links and capture one mobile error case. Use the career block for CV updates instead of adding a separate full workday.", "Commit evidence and check the weekly acceptance gate."], "page": 27}, {"n": 7, "title": "30-minute mentor review, then rest", "tasks": ["Record hours, evidence, one lesson and the next action on the weekly review sheet."], "page": 27}], "gate": "Android APK or reproducible build, a short video and a mobile failure-case checklist exist; all three projects have readable documentation and real evidence.", "fallback": "Reuse the existing backend; postpone voice, offline AI and Play Store release.", "page": 26}, {"n": 12, "title": "Interviews and applications", "intro": "Convert the portfolio into interviews while closing skill gaps.", "days": [{"n": 1, "title": "Coding mock", "tasks": ["Practice Python collections, async pitfalls, HTTP and SQL.", "Complete one timed coding exercise and explain complexity and edge cases.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 28}, {"n": 2, "title": "RAG system design", "tasks": ["Design a multi-tenant knowledge assistant on paper.", "Cover ingestion, access controls, retrieval, evaluation, latency, cost, deletion and failures.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 28}, {"n": 3, "title": "Agent mock", "tasks": ["Explain state, checkpointing, approvals, idempotency and MCP boundaries.", "Debug one failing scenario from your own traces.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 28}, {"n": 4, "title": "Behavioral stories", "tasks": ["Prepare 5 STAR stories from real Android/software experience: ownership, incident, tradeoff, ambiguity and collaboration.", "Add one AI project learning story.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 29}, {"n": 5, "title": "Applications", "tasks": ["Submit 5 tailored applications and seek 2 relevant referrals or conversations.", "Tailor the top CV bullets and include the most relevant project demo.", "Save a reproducible example or test result and note one decision in the daily log."], "page": 29}, {"n": 6, "title": "Catch-up and ship", "tasks": ["Finish the highest-priority unfinished core task; add no new topic.", "Review mock feedback, check the certification readiness gate and plan the next 4 weeks from feedback and application funnel data.", "Commit evidence and check the weekly acceptance gate."], "page": 29}, {"n": 7, "title": "30-minute mentor review, then rest", "tasks": ["Record hours, evidence, one lesson and the next action on the weekly review sheet."], "page": 29}], "gate": "You can demo without a script, explain your metrics and failures, complete two mocks and maintain a real application tracker. Continue applying beyond week 12.", "fallback": "Choose two strong mocks and tailored applications; postpone optional certification.", "page": 28}], "projects": [{"id": 0, "title": "Enterprise RAG Assistant", "intro": "A private company knowledge assistant that answers from approved documents and shows supporting sources. Build with public or synthetic material; never publish employer documents.", "sections": {"Minimum release": "Authenticated document ingestion, search and question-answer endpoints; source metadata survives parsing and chunking. PostgreSQL + pgvector, explicit ownership filters, repeatable indexing and deletion. Use a separate embedding provider/model if needed; budget embeddings independently from generation. Answers with validated source IDs, readable source previews and abstention when evidence is insufficient. A reproducible 40-question benchmark split before tuning, baseline comparison, adversarial cases and per-request usage/latency.", "Suggested structure": "Client -> authenticated FastAPI -> authorized retrieval -> context builder -> Claude -> validated answer + citations. Ingestion: upload -> parser -> chunks -> embeddings -> vector index. Enforce access controls in retrieval and source endpoints, not only in prompts.", "Suggested project targets, not promised results": "Aim for recall@5 >= 0.80 on answerable held-out questions, >= 90% supported citations in a manual audit and correct abstention on all 3 held-out unanswerable cases as a small-sample learning target. Report raw counts and denominators; small test sets have large uncertainty. If you miss a target, publish the result and diagnosis.", "Reviewer evidence": "README; architecture diagram; dataset provenance; labeled fixtures; evaluation command and report; demo video; failure analysis; threat model. Show one unsupported question and one attempted prompt injection during the demo.", "Stretch only after core completion": "Hybrid retrieval, reranking, incremental indexing or multilingual questions. Fine-tuning and Kubernetes are outside the 12-week core scope."}, "criteria": ["Authenticated document ingestion, search and question-answer endpoints; source metadata survives parsing and chunking.", "PostgreSQL + pgvector, explicit ownership filters, repeatable indexing and deletion. Use a separate embedding provider/model if needed; budget embeddings independently from generation.", "Answers with validated source IDs, readable source previews and abstention when evidence is insufficient.", "A reproducible 40-question benchmark split before tuning, baseline comparison, adversarial cases and per-request usage/latency."], "page": 30, "weeks": [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10]}, {"id": 1, "title": "Agentic AI Platform", "intro": "A support workflow that reads a request, checks policy, proposes a response and creates a sandbox ticket only after approval. A small dependable platform is enough.", "sections": {"Minimum release": "LangGraph workflow with typed state, routing and durable checkpoints; endpoints to start, inspect and resume runs. Three local tools with explicit input/output contracts; two exposed via MCP in week 9. Approval tied to the exact write arguments; owner-scoped permissions; idempotency keys and a visible audit trail. Step/time/cost budgets, tool timeouts, safe retry policy and recoverable failure states. 20 scenario fixtures covering success, denial, outage, malicious tool output, resume and repeated requests.", "Evaluation contract": "Report completed-correct / total scenarios, tool selection/argument correctness, duplicate side effects and approval bypass attempts. Require zero unauthorized writes and zero duplicate tickets in the defined safety fixtures before demoing write actions. These checks do not prove general security.", "Architecture decisions to defend": "Why a graph instead of a simple function chain? What state is persisted? Which steps can replay? What does approval authorize? What happens if a tool succeeds but its response is lost? Why does an MCP connection not grant blanket permission?", "Reviewer evidence": "State diagram, sanitized trace, scenario report, checkpoint/resume demo, tool contracts, approval audit example and a known-limitations section. Use sandbox writes; add real integrations only after explicit authorization and appropriate access controls."}, "criteria": ["LangGraph workflow with typed state, routing and durable checkpoints; endpoints to start, inspect and resume runs.", "Three local tools with explicit input/output contracts; two exposed via MCP in week 9.", "Approval tied to the exact write arguments; owner-scoped permissions; idempotency keys and a visible audit trail.", "Step/time/cost budgets, tool timeouts, safe retry policy and recoverable failure states.", "20 scenario fixtures covering success, denial, outage, malicious tool output, resume and repeated requests."], "page": 31, "weeks": [7, 8, 9, 10]}, {"id": 2, "title": "Android + AI", "intro": "An Android knowledge companion using your existing backend. This is the bridge between your proven mobile experience and your new AI engineering skills.", "sections": {"Minimum release": "Kotlin/Compose screen for question entry, answer display, source references and session state. Lifecycle-aware coroutines, cancellation, loading/error states and readable long answers. Backend-issued authentication; no provider credentials in the APK; clear logout and session-expiry behavior. Network and UI checks covering rotation, slow response, offline state, repeated tap and unauthorized source access. APK or build instructions, screenshots and a 90-second demo that shows both value and recovery from an error.", "Keep the backend as the authority": "The mobile app sends the user request and session token. The backend decides document access, model usage, retrieval and rate limits. Hide privileged operations from the mobile client and validate every request server-side.", "What makes this credible": "Explain streaming versus full-response tradeoffs, mobile cancellation versus server job lifetime, session expiry and source display. Show how your Android architecture and testing skills improved the AI product experience.", "Avoid expanding the scope": "Voice, on-device inference, offline retrieval and a full Play Store release are optional future work. A focused, reliable client demonstrates more than several unfinished AI features."}, "criteria": ["Kotlin/Compose screen for question entry, answer display, source references and session state.", "Lifecycle-aware coroutines, cancellation, loading/error states and readable long answers.", "Backend-issued authentication; no provider credentials in the APK; clear logout and session-expiry behavior.", "Network and UI checks covering rotation, slow response, offline state, repeated tap and unauthorized source access.", "APK or build instructions, screenshots and a 90-second demo that shows both value and recovery from an error."], "page": 32, "weeks": [11]}], "reference": [{"page": 3, "text": "AI ENGINEER / 12-WEEK MENTOR ROADMAP\n3\nMENTOR WORKBOOK\nHow to use this roadmap\nBuild on your existing strengths\nKeep your Android identity: API integration, architecture, asynchronous work, testing, lifecycle management\nand product delivery transfer directly. Your new focus is Python services, model behavior, retrieval quality and\nreliable tool execution. You are changing specialization, not restarting your career.\nOne shared budget: 14-16 hours/week\nBlock\nAction\n10 min\nRecall and choose today's outcome.\n20 min\nRead only the documentation needed for the task.\n70 min\nImplement one bounded slice.\n20 min\nVerify, commit and record evidence.\nDays 1-5: 2 hours/day. Day 6: up to 2 hours for catch-up and shipping. Day 7: 30-minute review, then rest.\nReserve another 1.5-3.5 hours for applications, interview practice and later certification. Total: 14-16 hours.\nThese activities share one budget; they are not extra homework.\nMinimum viable day: 45 minutes\nSpend 5 minutes recalling, 30 minutes building one small change, and 10 minutes checking and logging it. If\nbench ends or client work resumes, use 8-10 hours/week and stretch each roadmap week across two\ncalendar weeks.\nMentor check-in\nReturn with: Week/Day; time spent; completed checklist; commit/demo link; one test or metric; blocker and\nexact error; what you can explain without help. Ask for a review and the next task. A mentor can adjust the\nplan when you check in; this PDF does not schedule reminders.\nWeekly rule\nDay 6 ships evidence. Day 7 is a 30-minute review plus rest or a short catch-up. Complete the core\nacceptance criteria before optional features. Track achievements only when the evidence exists.\n"}, {"page": 4, "text": "AI ENGINEER / 12-WEEK MENTOR ROADMAP\n4\nMENTOR WORKBOOK\nDefault stack and scope boundaries\nArea\nDefault path for this learning plan\nPython/backend\nOne supported Python version; venv + pip, FastAPI, Pydantic, httpx and pytest. Pin working\ndependencies.\nPersistence\nPostgreSQL + pgvector; SQLAlchemy + Alembic for data access and migrations. Qdrant is\noptional later.\nModels\nClaude for generation and tools. Use a small sentence-transformers embedding model locally;\nrecord its exact ID and revision. OpenAI adapter is optional.\nAuthentication\nFor the private learning demo, follow FastAPI OAuth2/password + JWT tutorial with password\nhashing and two synthetic users. No public signup. Reassess auth before real users.\nAgent/MCP\nLangGraph for stateful workflows; official Python MCP SDK for two local read-only tools via stdio.\nRemote HTTP deployment is optional.\nDeployment\nDocker Compose locally. Default staging path: Render container service plus managed\nPostgreSQL, after checking current pricing and pgvector support. Local video demo is the\nno-hosting fallback.\nObservability\nStart with structured JSON logs and correlation IDs. Add OpenTelemetry traces if time remains;\nchoose one viewer, not several.\nAndroid\nKotlin + Compose + coroutines, calling the same FastAPI backend. Ship a focused client.\nCore versus optional\nCore: one provider, one corpus, vector retrieval, source-linked answers, owner isolation, measured evaluation,\none bounded agent, local MCP tools and an Android client. Optional: provider comparison, hybrid retrieval,\nreranking, streaming polish, public hosting and advanced telemetry.\nDay 6 adds no new topic. Finish the acceptance gate or use the reduced fallback stated for the week. If a core\ngate still fails, extend the week. Keep quality and permission checks; cut features first.\n"}, {"page": 33, "text": "AI ENGINEER / 12-WEEK MENTOR ROADMAP\n33\nMENTOR WORKBOOK\nQuality, security and operations scorecard\nArea\nRelease evidence\nEvaluation\nVersion prompts/model/config; keep held-out fixtures separate; compare against a simple\nbaseline; inspect failures manually.\nAuthorization\nTwo-user access tests for documents, retrieval, source links, agent runs and resume endpoints.\nPrompt injection\nTreat retrieved text and tool results as data; test hostile instructions; enforce tool permissions in\ncode.\nSecrets and privacy\nNo keys in repo, APK, image or logs; use synthetic data; redact traces; document deletion and\nretention.\nTool safety\nValidate arguments and destinations; allowlist tools; approve writes; use idempotency and\nbounded execution.\nAPI resilience\nTimeouts, bounded retries, request limits, cancellation and clear provider error behavior.\nObservability\nRequest/run ID, node/tool name, status, latency, usage and estimated cost; avoid storing full\nsensitive prompts by default.\nDeployment\nTLS, managed secrets, pinned dependencies, health checks, database migrations and tested\nbackup/rollback.\nBudget before experiments\nSet a personal weekly spending cap before real calls. Estimate: model input tokens x current input rate +\noutput tokens x output rate + embeddings + reranking + hosting/storage. Rates differ by provider and model;\ncheck live pricing. Start with a tiny dataset, mock calls in CI and stop app-side requests when the cap is\nreached.\nA useful experiment log\nDate | commit | dataset/version | prompt/model/version | change | quality counts | latency sample size |\nestimated cost | failure examples | keep/revert decision.\n"}, {"page": 34, "text": "AI ENGINEER / 12-WEEK MENTOR ROADMAP\n34\nMENTOR WORKBOOK\nInterview preparation | weekly practice\nWhen\nPractice and evidence\nWeeks 1-2\nTwo 20-minute Python/SQL drills per week. Explain typing, mutable defaults, async vs threads,\nvalidation, dependency injection and joins.\nWeeks 3-4\nExplain the model request lifecycle, structured output, tool calling, context budgets, embeddings\nand retrieval. Draw the RAG pipeline from memory.\nWeeks 5-6\nOne 30-minute RAG design mock each week. Defend chunking, recall@k, groundedness,\nabstention and leakage-free evaluation.\nWeeks 7-9\nOne agent debugging/design mock each week. Cover state, retries, idempotency, approvals, tool\ntrust and MCP transport/auth boundaries.\nWeeks 10-12\nOne coding mock + one system design/behavioral mock per week. Record answers and act on the\nweakest area.\nQuestions you should be able to answer\nWhen would you avoid an agent? How do you know a RAG change improved quality? Can a citation be valid\nbut misleading? What happens when the model or vector DB is unavailable? How do you enforce tenant\nisolation? How do you prevent duplicate tool side effects? How would you reduce cost without losing\nacceptable quality?\nMock interview rubric: 0-2 per dimension\nScore problem clarification, technical correctness, tradeoffs, failure/security handling and clarity. 0 = missing, 1\n= partial, 2 = convincing with evidence. Target >= 8/10 across two mocks, with no zero in correctness or failure\nhandling. This is a practice gate, not a prediction of hiring outcomes.\nBehavioral preparation\nUse real examples from Android/software delivery. For each STAR story, capture the constraints, your own\ncontribution, the decision, the outcome and what you would change. Do not invent production AI experience\nfrom portfolio prototypes.\n"}, {"page": 35, "text": "AI ENGINEER / 12-WEEK MENTOR ROADMAP\n35\nMENTOR WORKBOOK\nPositioning | CV and LinkedIn\nSuggested headline\nSoftware Engineer | Android + Python/FastAPI | Building RAG and agentic AI applications\nPositioning summary to adapt\nSoftware Engineer with Android product delivery experience, expanding into AI backend engineering. Building\nevaluated RAG services and controlled agent workflows with Python, FastAPI and Claude, with a focus on API\nreliability, access controls and mobile integration.\nCV structure\nKeep your real job titles and dates. Lead with relevant engineering achievements; add a clearly labeled AI\nportfolio/projects section. Put Python, FastAPI, RAG and evaluation above tools you have only sampled. Add\ndemo and GitHub links near each project.\nEvidence-backed bullet patterns\nBuilt an authenticated RAG assistant over [N] public/synthetic documents using FastAPI and pgvector;\nachieved [measured recall@5] on [N] held-out answerable queries, with source-linked responses.\nImplemented a checkpointed support agent with approval-gated writes and idempotency; passed [X/Y] defined\nscenarios, including replay, rejection and provider timeout.\nCreated an Android client for the AI backend with lifecycle-aware cancellation and source navigation; verified\n[specific device/network cases].\nReplace every bracket with verified evidence or omit that claim. State sample size and test conditions; never\nimply prototype metrics came from customers.\nHow to discuss bench time\nDescribe it neutrally as a period between assignments used for focused upskilling and portfolio delivery. Keep\nemployer status accurate. If permitted, seek an internal AI proof of concept or backend assignment alongside\nexternal applications.\nLinkedIn content cadence\nPost one useful artifact every 1-2 weeks: a demo, measured retrieval experiment, failure analysis or Android\nintegration lesson. Link the evidence and explain one tradeoff. List certification only once earned; training\ncompletion is not certification.\n"}, {"page": 36, "text": "AI ENGINEER / 12-WEEK MENTOR ROADMAP\n36\nMENTOR WORKBOOK\nApplication timeline and tracking\nWhen\nAction\nWeek 1\nCollect 10-15 relevant job descriptions. Extract repeated skills, seniority, language/location needs\nand evidence expected. Pick two target role families.\nWeeks 2-3\nRefresh CV basics and LinkedIn. Have 2 exploratory conversations/week with colleagues or\nrecruiters; ask about actual project needs.\nWeeks 4-5\nShare the first RAG demo and explore internal opportunities. Start 2-3 targeted applications/week\nwhere your existing software background fits.\nWeeks 6-8\nAim for 2-4 tailored applications/week and one short interview mock; include a relevant\nconversation when time permits. Lead with the RAG report; update links as the agent matures.\nWeeks 9-12\nAim for 3-5 tailored applications/week when suitable roles exist; increase only if the time budget\nallows. Match each application to the strongest project; follow up after 7-10 business days when\nappropriate.\nAfter week 12\nContinue the pipeline. Every 2 weeks inspect response rates and feedback; improve the weakest\nevidence rather than adding more frameworks.\nApplication tracker columns\nCompany | role/link | fit notes | contact/referral | application date | CV version | project used | stage | follow-up\ndate | feedback | next action.\nDiagnose the funnel\nFew replies: check role fit, CV clarity and demo accessibility. Recruiter screens but no technical progress:\nimprove your transition story and fundamentals. Technical rejections: record the exact gap and practice it.\nAvoid drawing strong conclusions from a handful of applications.\nChoose roles that value your bridge\nPrioritize AI application/backend roles, product teams adding AI features and Android roles with AI\nintegrations. Research-heavy ML roles may require deeper statistics, training and research experience beyond\nthis roadmap. Preserve your existing software seniority while being candid about new AI skills.\n"}, {"page": 37, "text": "AI ENGINEER / 12-WEEK MENTOR ROADMAP\n37\nMENTOR WORKBOOK\nClaude Architect certification | when and why\nRecommended timing: assess readiness in week 10; consider sitting\nClaude Certified Architect - Foundations in weeks 12-14, after the core\nportfolio is usable. This timing is mentor guidance, not an official\nprerequisite.\nProgram information / reconfirm before booking\nEarlier official-page results listed the Foundations Architect exam as 60 questions, 120 minutes and USD 125,\nwith 12-month validity in the previously retrieved listing. Pearson VUE previously described access through\norganizations in the Claude Partner Network. A fresh page fetch was unavailable during this revision, so treat\nthose details as provisional. Confirm eligibility, current exam guide, fee and delivery through the official links\nbefore paying. [1, 2]\nReadiness gate\nYou can build and explain a Claude API service, structured responses and a bounded tool loop.\nYour RAG evaluation report and agent approval/resume demo are complete.\nYou can defend context management, reliability, MCP trust boundaries, cost and deployment choices.\nYou have studied the current official blueprint, including Claude Code configuration/workflows, which the\nproject plan alone does not fully cover.\nYou can explain missed practice questions and complete two timed practice sessions without relying on\nmemorized answers.\nYour eligibility, budget and booking route are confirmed through Anthropic Academy / Pearson VUE.\nConcrete certification practice / optional career block\nWeek 10: 45 minutes to map the current blueprint to your evidence; 45 minutes to configure project\ninstructions and scoped permissions in a disposable Claude Code repo. Week 11: 45 minutes for context\nmanagement and a harmless hook/workflow exercise; 45 minutes to test scope boundaries and review\nchanges. Week 12: two 45-minute timed scenario sessions plus 30 minutes reviewing mistakes. Use official\navailable material; do not assume practice scores predict the exam.\nPreparation allocation\nWeeks 3-9: keep notes mapped to API, prompts, context, tools and agents. Weeks 10-12: reserve at most\n90-120 minutes/week inside the shared career budget for blueprint gaps and official training; reduce optional\nproject work. If more time is needed, add 1-2 weeks after the roadmap.\nIf access is unavailable or readiness is weak\nContinue the portfolio and applications. Complete available official learning modules and accurately list them\nas training. Delay the exam instead of delaying your job search. Consider Developer Foundations only after\ncomparing its current blueprint with target jobs; do not collect multiple credentials by default.\nOfficial sources: [1] Anthropic Academy exam page; [2] Pearson VUE Claude program. Links on the resources page.\n"}, {"page": 41, "text": "AI ENGINEER / 12-WEEK MENTOR ROADMAP\n41\nMENTOR WORKBOOK\nDaily log | reusable fillable sheet\nSave a copy of this PDF for each review period, or duplicate this page in your PDF editor. Keep the detailed engineering\nlog in your repository.\nWeek / day / date / planned minutes\nOne concrete outcome\nI built and can demonstrate the change.\nI checked one meaningful failure case.\nI saved evidence and chose the next task.\nCommit or demo link / test result / sample size\nWhat I learned / decision and reason\nBlocker / what I tried / specific mentor question\nActual minutes / next smallest action\n"}, {"page": 42, "text": "AI ENGINEER / 12-WEEK MENTOR ROADMAP\n42\nMENTOR WORKBOOK\nResources and next steps\nUse official documentation to answer the current implementation\nquestion, then return to the project. Avoid completing entire courses\nwhen one focused chapter is enough. Reference links collected 28\nOctober 2026; availability may vary. Certification links could not be\nre-fetched for this revision.\nPython tutorial - Weeks 1-2: syntax, modules, exceptions and classes.\nFastAPI tutorial - Weeks 2 and 10: validation, dependencies, security and testing.\nClaude tool use - Week 3: tool contracts and execution flow.\npgvector repository - Weeks 4-6: vector storage, indexing and query behavior.\nLangGraph / LangChain OSS overview - Weeks 7-8: stateful orchestration and framework choice.\nMCP documentation and security - Week 9: transport, authorization and tool trust boundaries.\nDocker getting started - Week 10: images, containers and reproducible services.\nAndroid architecture guidance - Week 11: lifecycle-aware mobile architecture.\n[1] Anthropic Academy: Architect Foundations - Certification blueprint and preparation; verify current details.\n[2] Pearson VUE: Claude certification program - Partner-network eligibility and exam scheduling information.\nYour next action: Day 1\nCreate the repository, run a small Python script and write the first daily log. Bring the result to your mentor\ncheck-in. Your goal is a repeatable learning-and-shipping rhythm with visible evidence every week.\n"}]};